00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.429 align:start position:0%
 
former CIA director Michael Hayden  believes the intelligence community is  under threat from outside forces  Hayden led the agency under President  George HW Bush and through the start of  the Obama administration retired the  retired Air Force general also ran the  National Security Agency from 1999 to  2005 Hayden's new book is called the  assault on intelligence American  national security in an age of lies it  argues that forces including the Trump  White House make the work of  intelligence officers harder but more  important than ever  general Hayden welcome we get to the  book in a minute but president Trump  said about Isis that that his  administration has done a great job  ridding the region of Isis but there has  now been this attack in Kabul a double  suicide attack where do things actually  stand today so the president has amped  up the pressure on Isis both in  Afghanistan and in Syria a few more  forces maybe a little more local  commander authority to get them some  things done on the battlefield folks  like me celebrate that I call it kind of  Obama plus you know a little more energy  a little more freedom of action but this  is a long-term battle this is not one  way you start spiking the football and  so you see in Syria we're not yet done  and the local commanders are trying to  say it's not time to turn the lights out  and here in Afghanistan actually John  Afghanistan is probably a condition to  be managed not a problem to be solved  we're gonna have to keep the level of  violence there down to an acceptable  level I think for a very long time we  are on the eve of a historic summit  between President Trump and the leader  of North Korea South Korea now just  saying that the North is willing to give  up its nuclear program if there can be  peace on the peninsula in the United  States vows not to attack do you really  believe that North Korea will give up  its program look I I want to be hopeful  that the profession demands me to be  realistic we have been pretty much at  this exact spot three other times under  three other administrations now that's  not my thing we shouldn't give it  another shot  that's not my saying that we've done  some good things the isolation of the  regime the economic pressure all good  the red  last summer may have made us a little  more dangerous than we had to be but  we're moving in a direction that might  give us an opportunity now one thing I I  point out in the book is what's the  intelligence backdrop to the American  negotiating position the intelligence  backdrop to to what it is we've been  doing and if you if you just look at  people currently in office and carefully  read what they say they make the point  that it would almost be irrational for  Kim to give up his weapons and anything  like the current strategic circumstances  so when we say denuclearization  I hope this Intelligence Estimate is  included in what our expectations are  and what our negotiating approach will  be and in addition to that we heard the  president's new national security  adviser yesterday on Face the Nation  talked about verification of that  denuclearization which is a further step  so sorry I think the arc and again I try  to suggest in the book these are wicked  problems and you don't need a president  saying I and I alone and it and they're  and they're still here because the other  guys were stupid and ineffective they're  still here because they're hard and  they're still going to be here after he  leaves in one way or another so again I  hope we have muted expected realistic  expectations here general the book is  called the assault on intelligence what  do you make of the president's  relationship with the intelligence  community at this point so it's had a  bit of a rocky road a good friend of  mine and I use this as an outline in the  book john mclaughlin says the  relationship has gone through four  stages all right first of all was  ignorance I mean he really didn't know  anything about the community that's not  uncommon but I think it was an extra  dose this time simply because he and  members of his team had no no experience  in government it quickly it quickly went  to confrontation because the first time  we had to meet him and discuss was over  an issue that other people were using to  challenge his legitimacy as president  the whole Russian thing we've now gotten  into a zone of what John calls  inevitability you can't live without it  all right and we're existing comfortably  uncomfortably this investigation on  which III know and that's why I said  comfortably uncomfortably some days or a  good day some days or bad days but the  core the core here in even in this  coexistence is the president's view  towards truth and reality and that's the  point I try to make in the book we've  had presidents who disagree with us  we've had presidents who lie we've not  had presidents for whom objective  reality doesn't seem to be compelling  but general people would push back and  say wait a minute in terms the  intelligence agencies you had directed  the CIA saying it was a slam dunk that  weapons of mass destruction there was  wireless warrantless wiretapping going  on for a long time there was not truth  about that I know that and the Senate  Intelligence Committee report on  waterboarding said there were all kinds  of untruths so there was an underlying  ground truth about untruths coming from  the intelligence it was John I get it  hard work but that's an argument about  the truth  all right that's an argument about what  constitutes objective reality I'm saying  over here we have a trend line that  seems to want to make decisions not on  objective reality cases in point the  Muslim man remember about a week out of  the gate the new administration because  why because we had an apocalyptic threat  from Syrian refugees and a absolutely  dystopian vetting system no reality  whatsoever and by the way you don't see  the Intel community even the ones  currently in government arguing for that  it was made based upon something else  not an argument over objective reality  what about the fact that the president  made his the choice to retaliate against  Syria based on an intelligence  assessment of their use of chemical  weapons when he put action behind it he  absolutely did and and and I see  evidence that when it comes to  implementing back to the necessity and  evitable 'ti the president goes to the  intelligence as to how to go do this  what I didn't see it may have happened I  I hope it did but what I what I didn't  see wasn't wasn't a question to the  intelligence community for the broader  context like why did he do that  is he scared or is he overconfident what  were the iranians thinking what were the  russians thinking Michael  and I emphasize this in the book Michael  Gerson has a great phrase for the  president and this is this is the real  challenge for intelligence Michael says  the president lives in the eternal now  there's little history there are a few  consequences the purpose of intelligence  is context it's all about history it's  all about consequences and hence the  challenge we have by the way LG talked  about I talked about the phases we went  from ignorance to OP to opposition to  acceptance inevitability phase four is  when the modal report finally comes out  and you think he'll accept it well  that's why phase four is different from  three we don't know what it is and we  don't know what the response will be but  right now the president has been  chipping away making war dead  legitimizing important institutions like  the Department of Justice and the FBI  can that spread again because he's done  it before  to the intelligence community and the  point I try to make in the book to  people still in government is in  addition to your to your own conscience  you know and being who you are you are  responsible for these institutions for  their survival  you cannot accommodate so much to the  peculiarities and quirks of any one  president that you D legitimize the  institution for history the people and  future presidents well general Hayden  always good to have you  lots hear about a lot of different hot  spots around the world including  Russia's continued disinformation  campaign and how they're trying to  influence our elections thank you so  much for being here we appreciate it  Congrats on the books the assault on  intelligence goes on sale tomorrow

